WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.240
Internal network abuse occurs when a user-controlled URL or destination influences a

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.340
server-side request. Features such as URL previews, webhook testing, file import,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.240
and document conversion can become SSRF entry points.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.140
Cloud metadata exploitation is a major SSRF scenario.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.160
If a workload can reach metadata endpoints,

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.940
attackers may attempt to obtain temporary credentials or environmental information.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.040
Trust boundary violations happen when applications treat user-supplied destinations as

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.500
safe. URL validation, protocol restrictions, redirect handling,

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.440
and DNS behavior need careful design.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.780
SSRF defenses work best when application validation is paired with network segmentation,

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.880
egress filtering, infrastructure protections,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.700
and monitoring for unusual server-initiated traffic.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.360
Use network segmentation and infrastructure protections.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:59.540
Validate URLs and restrict destinations with allow lists where possible.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.760
Apply egress filtering and monitor unusual outbound requests.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:10.220
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC6.6 - Network security protections;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.120
CC7.1 - Threat and vulnerability management.
